'\" t
.\" dvbrx(8): DVB receiver on a software radio
.TH DVBRX 8 2026-11-06 "dvbrx 0.1" "dvbrx manual"
.SH NAME
dvbrx \- real-time DVB-S/S2/S2X/C/T/T2 receiver on a software radio, exposed as /dev/dvb
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY dvbrx
.RB ( \-u
.RI [ DRIVER :] ARGS
.B \-f
.I HZ
|
.B \-F
.IR FILE )
.B \-s
.I SYM/S
.RI [ options ]
.YS
.SY dvbrx
.RB ( \-u
.RI [ DRIVER :] ARGS
|
.B \-F
.IR FILE )
.B \-S
.I HZ
.B \-m
.IR CHANNEL ...\&
.RB [ \-f
.IR HZ ]
.YS
.SY dvbrx
.B \-d
.I N
.RB [ \-n
.I COUNT
.RB [ \-W
.IR HZ ]]
.RB ( \-u
.RI [ DRIVER :] ARGS
|
.B \-F
.IR FILE )
.RI [ options ]
.YS
.SY dvbrx
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help " | " \-\-version
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dvbrx
demodulates and decodes in real time the DVB-S, DVB-S2 (and S2X), DVB-C,
DVB-T and DVB-T2 standards from a software radio (USRP B2xx through UHD,
bladeRF, HackRF) or from an IQ file, and produces the MPEG-TS transport
stream.
.PP
It has three modes:
.TP
.B standalone
one channel, tuned on the command line; the TS goes to standard output,
to a file or over UDP
.RB ( \-o ).
.TP
.B multichannel
several multiplexes extracted from one wideband capture
.RB ( \-m
repeated), each to its own output.
.TP
.B /dev/dvb bridge
.RB ( \-d
.IR N )
with the
.B dvbrx
kernel module, the daemon serves
.BI /dev/dvb/adapter N
like an ordinary DVB card: applications (tvheadend, dvbv5-zap, VLC,
Kaffeine...) tune the frontend, dvbrx receives these tuning requests through
.BI /dev/dvbrx N\c
, tunes the radio and sends the TS back into the demux. This is the mode of
the
.BR dvbrxd@ \fIN\fR
service.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Radio
.TP
.BR \-u " [\fIDRIVER\fR:]\fIARGS\fR"
Radio and device to open.
.I DRIVER
is
.B uhd
(default; UHD
.IR ARGS ,
for example
.BR type=b200,serial=31A5B7C ),
.B bladerf
(libbladeRF device string, for example
.BR *:serial=0a0896e9813f43d29e2ff2278014dfb1 )
or
.B hackrf
(serial number, or its end, as shown by
.BR hackrf_info ).
Empty
.IR ARGS :
the first device found. With several devices, always give the serial
number.
.TP
.BI \-F " FILE"
IQ file (interleaved 32-bit floating-point complex samples) instead of a
radio. In bridge mode, it is replayed in a loop at the real rate and the
frequency is ignored.
.TP
.BR \-G " \fIDB\fR | " auto [: \fIMAX\fR]
Receive gain (default 40 dB).
.B auto
first sets it for a mean level of \-24 dBFS (and lowers it at once if the
ADC clips), then, once the receiver is locked, moves it in 3 dB steps
towards the gain that gives the best SNR;
.I MAX
caps it. Recommended on cable and antenna.
.TP
.BI \-A " INPUT"
Input of the device: UHD
.B TX/RX
or
.BR RX2 ,
bladeRF
.B RX1
(default) or
.BR RX2 .
The HackRF has only one; its antenna power always stays off.
.TP
.BI \-B " HZ"
Width of the analogue filter (default: occupied width of the channel
+ 15%, or the whole capture in multichannel mode; 0: UHD's choice).
.TP
.BI \-M " HZ"
Master clock (UHD).
.TP
.BI \-S " HZ"
Sample rate. Standalone mode: default 2 samples per symbol, 1.12 times the
elementary rate in OFDM; multichannel: rate of the capture. Bridge mode:
maximum rate (default: that of the radio).
.SS Channel (standalone mode)
.TP
.BI \-f " HZ"
Centre frequency; on satellite, the intermediate frequency after the LNB.
.TP
.BI \-s " SYM/S"
Symbol rate (DVB-S, S2, C).
.TP
.BI \-r " ROLLOFF"
Roll-off (default 0.2).
.TP
.BI \-g " GOLD"
PL scrambling gold code (default 0);
.BI r N
: root code.
.TP
.BI \-i " ISI"
DVB-S2 input stream (default: all; over UDP, each on PORT + ISI); in
DVB-T2, the PLP.
.TP
.BI \-l " RATE"
DVB-S (first generation):
.BR auto ,
.BR 1/2 ,
.BR 2/3 ,
.BR 3/4 ,
.B 5/6
or
.BR 7/8 .
.TP
.BI \-c " QAM"
DVB-C:
.BR auto ,
16, 32, 64, 128 or 256 (roll-off 0.15).
.TP
.BI \-T " MHZ"
DVB-T, channel width (5, 6, 7 or 8; no
.BR \-s ).
.TP
.BI \-2 " MHZ"
DVB-T2, channel width;
.B \-i
selects the PLP.
.TP
.BI \-x " HZ"
Channel offset by
.I HZ
in a capture at
.BR \-S .
.TP
.BI \-D " SYMBOLS"
Half-length of the matched filter (default: depends on the roll-off).
.TP
.BI \-o " DEST"
TS output:
.B \-
(standard output, default), a file, or
.BI udp:// HOST : PORT\c
\&.
.TP
.B \-R
File: read at the real rate, in a loop.
.SS Multichannel
.TP
.BI \-m " CHANNEL"
One multiplex of the capture (repeatable option). Keys separated by
commas:
.B f
(Hz),
.B sys
.RB ( s ,
.BR s2 ,
.BR c ,
.BR t ,
.BR t2 ),
.B sr
(sym/s),
.B bw
(MHz),
.BR ro ,
.BR qam ,
.B fec
(DVB-S),
.BR isi / plp ,
.BR gold ,
.B o
(output). For example
.BR "\-m f=646e6,sys=t2,bw=8,o=udp://127.0.0.1:1234" .
.B \-f
sets the centre of the capture (default: middle of the channels).
.SS /dev/dvb bridge mode
.TP
.BI \-d " N"
Serves
.BI /dev/dvbrx N\c
, hence
.BI /dev/dvb/adapter N\c
\&.
.TP
.BI \-n " COUNT"
Serves adapters
.IR N ", " N +1...
from a single wideband capture (B210, bladeRF); each adapter has its own
receiver.
.TP
.BI \-W " HZ"
Rate of this capture (default 30.72e6).
.TP
.BI \-L " LNB"
LNB controller (13/18 V, 22 kHz, DiSEqC):
.B none
(default),
.BI serial: DEVICE
or
.BI udp: HOST : PORT\c
\&.
.TP
.BI \-C " PATH"
Control socket for
.BR dvbrx\-web (8)
(default
.BI /run/dvbrx/dvbrx\- N .sock\c
;
.B \(dq\(dq
: none).
.TP
.BI \-P " DB"
Input power estimate: dBm at 0 dBFS and 0 dB of gain (default 0).
.TP
.BI \-U " MB/S"
USB bandwidth shared by the radios on the same bus (default: 350 on USB 3,
42 on USB 2; 0: no sharing), see
.BR "USB BANDWIDTH" .
.SS Common
.TP
.BI \-t " N"
LDPC decoding threads (default 2).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Help.
.TP
.B \-\-version
The version.
.SH RADIOS
.TP
.B USRP B210 (UHD)
Up to 61.44 Msps, 70 MHz to 6 GHz. With timestamps: samples lost on the USB are replaced by
zeros, and the receivers keep their synchronisation.
.TP
.B bladeRF 2.0 micro (libbladeRF)
Same performance as the B210 (same AD9361). The FPGA must match
libbladeRF (FPGA 0.15.3 with libbladeRF 2024.05; with a newer FPGA, the
stream delivers only a quarter of the samples); dvbrx shows its version at
startup.
.TP
.B HackRF One (libhackrf)
20 Msps at most, 8-bit samples, clearly less sensitive than a B210 (6 to
7 dB measured behind a hub); a single receive chain (no
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-m ).
It is sensitive to its USB power supply: behind an unpowered hub, the
phase of its oscillator can jump periodically (errors in DVB-S and DVB-C
whatever the SNR); plug it in directly or into a powered hub.
.PP
In bridge mode, the sample rate is 2 samples per symbol if the radio can do
it, and at least symbol rate \(mu (1 + roll-off) / 0.9: the signal must stay
within 90% of the band. Hence the maximum symbol rates:
.TS
l l l l.
	DVB-S (0.35)	DVB-S2 (0.20 / 0.05)	DVB-C (0.15)
B210, bladeRF	41	46 / 52.7	48 Msym/s
HackRF	13.3	15 / 17.1	15.6 Msym/s
.TE
.PP
An automatic DVB-S2 roll-off is counted as 0.35. The daemon reports the
maximum for roll-off 0.05 in the frontend information
.RB ( FE_GET_INFO ,
.IR symbol_rate_max ;
in multichannel mode, from the usable band of the capture): the kernel
refuses at once, with
.BR EINVAL ,
a DVB-S, S2 or C tuning above it, and tvheadend moves to another free
tuner. A tuning between the two limits is refused by the daemon (log:
\(lqno usable sample rate\(rq): the application sees no signal.
.PP
If the radio no longer answers (unplugged), the daemon stops (exit code 3)
instead of insisting; systemd restarts it and it reopens the radio when it
comes back. A radio missing at startup is opened at the first tuning.
.PP
B210 and bladeRF (AD9361): between 138 and 250 MHz, signals at three times
the tuned frequency (414 to 750 MHz) fold back into the band; a low-pass
filter around 300 MHz in front of the input is needed for this band.
.SH FRONTEND AND TVHEADEND
Each adapter has a DVB-S/S2, a DVB-C and a DVB-T/T2 frontend (one at a
time). At startup, the daemon opens the radio and gives the frontend its
name, with the model and the serial number:
.RS
.EX
dvbrx B210 31A5B7C
dvbrx bladeRF 2.0 xA4 0a0896e9813f43d29e2ff2278014dfb1
dvbrx HackRF One 334c64dc39395367
.EE
.RE
.PP
tvheadend reads this name when it discovers the adapter and derives its
configuration identifier from it: each radio keeps its own. A new radio
comes with its frontends disabled: enable them and assign them the networks
(Configuration > DVB Inputs > TV adapters). The service is
.B Type=notify
and ordered before tvheadend, so that the name is in place when tvheadend
starts; a radio swapped while tvheadend is running only appears after
tvheadend restarts.
.PP
Tick
.B Power save
on the dvbrx frontends: the radio is released a few seconds after viewing
ends. With several radios, a higher
.B priority
on the most sensitive one makes it chosen first.
.SH SEVERAL RADIOS
One daemon per radio, each on its own adapter:
.RS
.EX
# /etc/modprobe.d/dvbrx.conf
options dvbrx adapters=2
# /etc/dvbrxd/0.conf
DVBRXD_ARGS="\-u bladerf:*:serial=0a0896e9... \-G auto \-t 3"
# /etc/dvbrxd/1.conf
DVBRXD_ARGS="\-u hackrf:334c64dc39395367 \-G auto \-t 2"

systemctl enable \-\-now dvbrxd@0 dvbrxd@1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Both receive different multiplexes at the same time; each radio is
designated by its serial number, the plug-in order does not matter. The
cost is that of two receivers (DVB-T2 or DVB-S2: one to two cores each).
.SH USB BANDWIDTH
USB is the bottleneck: an AD9361 radio sends 4 bytes per sample
(246 MB/s at 61.44 Msps), a HackRF 2 (40 MB/s at 20 Msps); a USB 3 bus
carries about 350 MB/s of payload, a USB 2 bus about 42. Different ports
do not mean different controllers: all the ports of a controller, and those
of the hubs plugged into it, share its bandwidth; an xHCI controller has a
USB 2 bus and a USB 3 bus on separate lines
.RB ( "lsusb \-t" ).
.PP
The daemons on the same bus share its bandwidth
.RI ( /run/dvbrx/usb\- N ).
On tuning, a daemon takes its full rate (2 samples per symbol) if it is
alone, otherwise what the others leave, without going below the minimum of
the channel; if it lacks room, the others step down through a new tuning
(about 1 s without TS for them) and keep that rate until their next tuning.
With no room after 2.5 s, it takes its minimum anyway.
.B \-U
.I MB/S
changes the budget of the bus (350 on USB 3, 42 on USB 2 by default; same
value for all the radios of a bus),
.B \-U 0
disables sharing. The captures of
.BR dvbrx\-web (8)
scans are not counted.
.PP
Stepping down costs SNR on a real signal (cable, neighbouring multiplexes:
2.4 dB in DVB-C 64-QAM at 1.28 samples per symbol): the daemon only does it
if the radio or the bus requires it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI /dev/dvbrx N
Control device of the module, opened by the daemon (only one at a time).
.TP
.BI /dev/dvb/adapter N
The DVB adapter seen by applications.
.TP
.BI /etc/dvbrxd/ N .conf
Options of the
.BR dvbrxd@ \fIN\fR
service
.RB ( DVBRXD_ARGS ).
.TP
.B /etc/modprobe.d/dvbrx.conf
Number of adapters of the module
.RB ( "options dvbrx adapters=" \fIN\fR,
1 to 8).
.TP
.BI /run/dvbrx/usb\- N
USB bandwidth used by daemon
.IR N ,
read by the other daemons on the same bus.
.TP
.BI /run/dvbrx/dvbrx\- N .sock
Control socket (JSON per line: status, constellation, probe, monitor,
capture, lnb), see
.IR doc/dvbrx\-web\-api.md .
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
# satellite: IF 1210 MHz, 27.5 Msym/s, to mpv
dvbrx \-u type=b200 \-f 1210e6 \-s 27.5e6 \-o udp://127.0.0.1:1234
# DVB-T2 8 MHz at 586 MHz on a bladeRF, PLP 0
dvbrx \-u bladerf: \-f 586e6 \-2 8 \-i 0 \-G auto \-o t2.ts
# two multiplexes from a 30.72 Msps capture
dvbrx \-u type=b200 \-G auto \-S 30.72e6 \e
    \-m f=646e6,sys=t2,bw=8,o=udp://127.0.0.1:1234 \e
    \-m f=655e6,sys=c,sr=6.9e6,qam=64,o=udp://127.0.0.1:1235
# /dev/dvb bridge by hand, with the LNB controller
sudo dvbrx \-d 0 \-u type=b200 \-G auto \-L serial:/dev/ttyACM0
.EE
